 - **Hard floors and the one re-ask (owner 2026-11-17, superseding "no model retry in v1").** After the number check, the kept script must have at least 110 (Brief), 200 (Balanced) or 300 (Detailed) words AND speak at least one of the card's own data numbers, meaning a number the card's PROSE or the learner's question states, not a name with digits (Q1, w_0), not a lone zero to ten (a card whose prose has none, only counting words, or only numbers inside a diagram, asks for no example; measured: six of the 12 bench cards had a JSON `version: 1` as their only number). A script that fails either gets exactly ONE re-ask: a single extra call to the same model with the same prompt, its first answer, and one user turn that names the failed rule(s) (`too short: N words after removing sentences with outside numbers; target L to H words; use the card's own numbers.` and/or `none of the card's own numbers (...) is spoken; say at least one of them aloud in the explanation`). Still under the floor after it, the script step is an error (`too_short`, the retry pill), never short audio. The card-number rule only triggers the re-ask: still missing after it, the script ships (outcome ok) with `check.exampleMissing: true`. The answer and the log carry `check` = `{ reasked, firstRawWords, firstStrippedWords, firstFailed?, rawWords, strippedWords, failed }` (the client keeps it as `narration.scriptCheck`); a provider failing on the re-ask hands the card to the next provider, which has no re-ask left.
 - **Style.** The script mirrors the card's own register, vocabulary and rhythm (the prompt's STYLE section); no added enthusiasm.
 - **Prompt.** `explanation-audio-script.js` `scriptSystem` is AUD-2's v1.1 (`docs/features/explain/checks/aud-2/prompt-v1.1.txt`, root causes in `why.md`) with these changes: the numbers rule is the owner's (only numbers that appear in the card or its visuals, never compute; this ends v1.1's tension with its reuse rule); the answer is `{script, transcript}`; Detailed stops at about 2 minutes; the LaTeX delimiter is written by code so the source carries no money-looking text.
-- **Number safety (deterministic, no model).** After the script returns, every number it speaks (digits, and spelled-out words such as "zero point three six eight") must be a number of the card (its prose, the learner's question, every label and value inside its diagrams, in digits or words). A sentence that speaks any other number, or a script sentence that carries a symbol (`= + * / ^ < > [ ] %` and the like), is removed from the script and the transcript together (paired by position when they have the same sentence count; else each on its own). No regenerate, no retry. One shared parser (`mentions`) reads the card and the script alike: digits, percents, spoken decimals ("point five"), fractions ("two thirds", "a quarter", "1/2"), hundred, thousand and million, "two and a half", signs ("minus two", "-2"), hyphenated compounds and spoken years; each mention carries its equivalent forms (36.8 percent is 36.8 and 0.368) and matches the card through any of them. Not numbers: a lone "one", ordinals, once, twice, double, a bare half, "hundreds of", the noun "point", subtraction ("n minus one"). The allowed set is what the learner can see: prose, question, labels, captions and rendered values, plus names with digits (Q1, w_0); a diagram spec's `version`, ids (`id`, `key`), node references and arrow end points (`from`, `to`, `source`, `target`, `index`) are left out. A lone zero to ten is free like the counting words (also when the transcript writes it as a digit). **Counting words** (owner 2026-11-17): when the card has any number, the spelled-out whole numbers two to ten standing alone ("two paths", "the other three") are not outside numbers; ordinals, twice, half, double, pair, a third and a quarter are not number words at all. Data stays counted: digits, decimals, percentages, spelled-out data ("zero point three six eight", "thirty seven", "twelve") and anything 11 and above. A card with no numbers at all follows the old rule (every spoken number is outside). The prompt says to use only the card's own numbers for examples; the rule lets invented small example sizes ("ten emails") pass, which the measurement showed (it saves about 5 words a Gemini script). The Fireworks fallback is also told: no symbols, LaTeX or Greek letters in the spoken script. Measured offline on AUD-2's real model outputs (the build README): Gemini V6 loses at least one sentence in 4 of 9 scripts (mean 1.2 of 12.4 sentences, 220 to 195 words), Fireworks V5 in 7 of 9 (2.4 of 14.2, 290 to 245 words).
